              "locator": "§3.4, Definition 3.10, printed pp. 34–35: tautological cotangent one-form."
            },
            {
              "title": "Ana Cannas da Silva, Lectures on Symplectic Geometry, complete lecture notes",
              "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20120413034139if_/http://www.math.ist.utl.pt:80/%7Eacannas/Books/symplectic.pdf",
              "locator": "Lecture 1, printed pp. 11–12: cotangent bundle canonical symplectic form."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Mathematical cotangent phase-space conventions; action units are interpretations assigned by a particle model, with no dynamics or measurement asserted."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Temporal chart with lapse, shift and positive slice metric",
        "statement": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $(M,g,T)$ be a smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-+++)$, with the geometric meaning fixed by [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the future-cone field called $N$ in that definition. A **temporal product chart** is a smooth coordinate map from an open product $I\\times\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3$ onto an open subset of $M$, with coordinates $(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$. We require $x^0$ to have length units and each $x^i$ to have length units in the physical interpretation.\n\nThe chart has **lapse-shift data** $(N,\\beta,h)$ when $N:I\\times\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)$, $\\beta^i:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$, and $h_{ij}:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ are smooth, $h_{ij}=h_{ji}$, and the matrix $(h_{ij})$ is positive definite at each point, with\n\n$$g=-N^2(dx^0)^2+h_{ij}(dx^i+\\beta^i dx^0)(dx^j+\\beta^j dx^0).$$\n\nWrite $h^{ij}$ for the inverse matrix. The normal field is\n\n$$n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i).$$\n\nDirect contraction gives $g(n,n)=-1$; require that $n$ is future-directed in the supplied time orientation. This field of observers is distinct from the coordinate vector $\\partial_0$, which may be spacelike. The coefficients $N,\\beta^i,h_{ij}$ are dimensionless when all four coordinates are in metres. This definition is local to the specified product chart; it asserts no global temporal foliation.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Eric Gourgoulhon, 3+1 Formalism and Bases of Numerical Relativity, complete lecture notes (arXiv:gr-qc/0703035)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/0703035",
              "locator": "Complete 220-page notes; §§3.3.1–3.3.3, printed pp. 41–44, and §§4.2.2–4.2.3, pp. 56–58; full relevant sections inspected."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Local lapse-shift chart and observer split for particle models; no global foliation or empirical claim is made."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-rpm-h-almost-regular-legendre",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Regular, hyperregular and almost-regular Legendre domains",
        "statement": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $\\mathcal D$ be an open subset of the time-dependent tangent bundle $\\mathbb R\\times TQ$ of a finite-dimensional smooth manifold $Q$, and let $L:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth. For fixed $t$, the **fibre derivative** is the map\n\n$$\\mathbb F L(t,q,v)=(t,q,p),\\qquad p(w)=\\left.\\frac{d}{ds}\\right|_{s=0}L(t,q,v+sw),$$\n\nas in [[def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian]]. In induced coordinates $p_i=\\partial L/\\partial v^i$.\n\nThe Lagrangian is **regular** at $(t,q,v)$ when the velocity Hessian $(\\partial^2L/\\partial v^i\\partial v^j)$ is invertible there; regularity gives only a local inverse branch. It is **hyperregular on a declared domain** when $\\mathbb F L$ is a diffeomorphism from that entire domain onto an open subset of $\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$.\n\nIt is **almost regular on a declared domain $\\mathcal D_0\\subseteq\\mathcal D$** when the following are all assumed: $\\mathbb F L|_{\\mathcal D_0}$ has constant rank; its image $N$ is an embedded submanifold of $\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$; its corestriction $\\mathcal D_0\\to N$ is a surjective submersion (its differential is surjective at every point); and every fibre of that corestriction is connected. These hypotheses are not consequences of singularity alone.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
          "def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Andrew Hanson, Tullio Regge and Claudio Teitelboim, Constrained Hamiltonian Systems, Part 1, Chapter I",
              "url": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstreams/0e1d11ee-2741-4da4-83ed-b7cabc0cd7d6/download",
              "locator": "Printed pp. 7–9: singular Lagrangians, primary constraints and the failure of unique velocity inversion."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Mathematical regularity package for Lagrangian models; it does not assert that a particular physical Lagrangian satisfies the package."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Lorentz background, particle quantities and parameter units",
        "statement": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nFix a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,T)$ with signature $(-+++)$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the supplied smooth timelike field selecting the future cone, called $N$ in that geometric definition. Use the distinction among a spacetime, a worldline, a pointwise observer, an observer congruence, a tetrad and a chart given in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. This definition does not make $g$ dynamical.\n\nIn this relativistic-particle model, $m>0$ is a fixed **primitive mass parameter** measured in kilograms, and $c>0$ is a fixed **primitive conversion parameter** measured in metres per second. They are parameters of the adopted physical formulation, not quantities derived by this definition. Coordinates $x^a$ in an SI chart have length units; a cotangent vector $p=p_a\\,dx^a$ has units of momentum, $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, when all four coordinates are in metres. The canonical one-form $p_a\\,dx^a$ therefore has action units $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Along a future-directed timelike curve with tangent $V=dx/d\\lambda$, set $s:=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$; then $d\\tau=s\\,d\\lambda/c$, independently of the increasing regular parameter. The length action $-mc\\int s\\,d\\lambda$ has action units. For a future unit observer $n$ and future causal covector $p$, define the model normal-observer energy and spatial momentum covector by\n\n$$E_n(p):=-c\\,p(n),\\qquad q_n(p):=p+p(n)n^\\flat,$$\n\nwhere $n^\\flat=g(n,\\cdot)$. The restriction of $g^{-1}$ to covectors annihilating $n$ is positive definite, so it supplies the norm of $q_n(p)$. These are quantity definitions and unit conventions; they do not identify canonical momentum with mechanical momentum, assert that an apparatus measured these values, or establish a dynamical law.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes (arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019",
              "locator": "Complete 238-page notes; Chapter 1, printed pp. 25–27, and Chapter 3, printed pp. 68–72; full relevant sections inspected."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "physical_scope": "Classical spinless test-particle quantities on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold. Mass and c are primitive parameters of this model; no particle action, force law, backreaction, empirical result, or universal identification with another framework is included.",
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Regular primary constraints and total generator",
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$ and let $(M^{2n},\\omega)$ be a smooth symplectic manifold. A finite family of smooth functions $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_r$ defined on an open neighborhood $U\\subseteq M$ is a **regular primary constraint family** on\n\n$$C:=\\{z\\in U:\\chi_1(z)=\\cdots=\\chi_r(z)=0\\}$$\n\nwhen $d\\chi_1(z),\\ldots,d\\chi_r(z)$ are linearly independent at every $z\\in C$. The regular-level theorem [[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]] then gives $C$ its embedded-submanifold structure and $T_zC=\\bigcap_a\\ker d\\chi_a(z)$. For functions $F,G$ on $U$, write $F\\approx G$ when $F|_C=G|_C$; thus $F\\approx0$ means that $F$ vanishes on $C$, without requiring it to vanish off $C$.\n\nGiven a smooth canonical Hamiltonian $H_c$ on $U$ and multiplier functions $u^a(t)$, the **total generator** is\n\n$$H_T(t,z):=H_c(t,z)+\\sum_{a=1}^r u^a(t)\\chi_a(z).$$\n\nThe functions $u^a$ are independent parameter functions; the definition does not assert that their equations determine them. If $H_c$ is an energy and $\\chi_a$ has units $[\\chi_a]$, then $u^a$ has units energy divided by $[\\chi_a]$, so every term in $H_T$ has the same units. A later constraint, if generated by consistency, is not declared primary by this definition.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
          "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold",
          "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Andrew Hanson, Tullio Regge and Claudio Teitelboim, Constrained Hamiltonian Systems, Part 1, Chapter I",
              "url": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstreams/0e1d11ee-2741-4da4-83ed-b7cabc0cd7d6/download",
              "locator": "Printed pp. 7–10: weak equality, primary and secondary constraints, multipliers and consistency."
            },
            {
              "title": "Ghanashyam Date, Lectures on Constrained Systems, §§3–4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1010.2062",
              "locator": "Printed pp. 12–22: regular constraint surfaces, multiplier consistency and first/second-class classification."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Mathematical local constraint-surface and total-generator conventions; no dynamics or physical constraint model is adopted."
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Canonical Poisson brackets and local Hamiltonian flow",
        "statement": "Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold as in [[def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space]], and write its cotangent phase space in induced coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$. With $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, define $X_H$ by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$ and define the Poisson bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=X_G(F)$. Then\n\n$$X_H=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial q^i}-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad \\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nThe bracket is coordinate independent and makes smooth functions a Poisson algebra. If $H_t$ is smooth on an open time-dependent domain $D\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$, each solution of $\\dot z=X_{H_t}(z)$ has local uniqueness and smooth dependence on initial data and parameters; on its actual interval,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}F(t,z(t))=\\partial_tF+\\{F,H_t\\}.$$\n\nNo completeness or global flow is asserted. If $H$ is smooth, each local time-$t$ flow map preserves ω.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
          "thm-smooth-functions-form-a-poisson-algebra",
          "thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-smooth-functions-form-a-poisson-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Andrew Hanson, Tullio Regge and Claudio Teitelboim, Constrained Hamiltonian Systems, Part 1",
              "url": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstreams/0e1d11ee-2741-4da4-83ed-b7cabc0cd7d6/download",
              "locator": "Chapter I, printed pp. 7–11: canonical Hamiltonian conventions in constrained mechanics."
            },
            {
              "title": "Ghanashyam Date, Lectures on Constrained Systems, arXiv:1010.2062",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1010.2062",
              "locator": "§§3–4, printed pp. 12–22: phase-space Poisson bracket convention."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "physical_scope": "Mathematical cotangent phase space; physical dimensions are interpreted only when a model assigns units to coordinates and observables.",
        "library": "physics"
