      {
        "id": "pthm-rpm-h-coordinate-versus-observer-energy",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Coordinate Hamiltonian and normal-observer energy differ by shift",
        "statement": "Under the hypotheses of [[thm-rpm-h-temporal-shell-reduction]], the future unit normal $n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i)$ measures positive particle energy\n\n$$E_n=-c\\,p(n)=c\\kappa,$$\n\nwhile the chart's reduced Hamiltonian is $H=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i)$. The normal-frame spatial velocity is $w^i=(\\dot q^i/c+\\beta^i)/N=h^{ij}p_j/\\kappa$, with $h_{ij}w^iw^j<1$ for $m>0$ and $=1$ for a nonzero massless state. Write $|\\beta|_h:=\\sqrt{h_{ij}\\beta^i\\beta^j}$ and $|p|_{h^{-1}}:=\\sqrt{h^{ij}p_ip_j}$. If $\\beta^2:=h_{ij}\\beta^i\\beta^j<N^2$, then $H>0$. If $\\beta^2>N^2$, $H$ can be negative even though $E_n>0$. Along a Hamiltonian solution, $dH/dt=\\partial_tH$; only explicit time independence implies its conservation.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
